exports[`the snippet > snapshot: config + snippet for the fixture > generator-notes.md 1`] = `
"# Consent tool config — example-shop.test

Generated 2026-10-06T12:00:00.000Z from run b2-fixture (complykit 0.0.0-test, knowledge base 0).

On what this scan saw: 1 page with its HTML inspected, 1 verified location (Germany), logged out, desktop Chromium, 1 run per visitor action. Pages not visited can load other tools.

## What the config contains

- **Necessary** (\`necessary\`): Cloudflare [none]
- **Analytics** (\`analytics\`): Google Analytics 4 [api: google-consent-mode]
- **Advertising** (\`advertising\`): Google Ads [platform], Meta Pixel [api: meta], TikTok Pixel [api: tiktok], tracker.test [none]
- Gate rules: 1
- GTM: GTM-XXXX01 — 1 tag that must carry a consent requirement
- Platform bridge: shopify
- Visitor location: platform

## Check before deploying

- tracker.test is not classified yet: held under the strictest category (advertising). Classify it in the site workspace and regenerate.
- tracker.test: how it gets onto the page was not established, so the config cannot hold it back. See “Needs a look” in the change list.
- Cloudflare is listed as necessary (cdn, per its knowledge-base entry) but behaved like a tracker in this scan (). Necessary tools are never held back: confirm its use is strictly necessary, or classify it in the workspace and regenerate.

## How it was decided

- Meta Pixel: an inline script — edit the existing tag in place (add type="text/plain" data-category="advertising"); the scan does not carry its code.
- Visitor location: from Shopify (customerPrivacy.getRegion()). Where Shopify does not report a region, the visitor is treated as opt-in.
- The snippet loads the tool from /complykit/v1/complykit-consent.js — a placeholder. Self-host complykit-consent.js and complykit-consent-ui.js in one folder and adjust the path; a third-party CDN URL means a request before consent.

This config describes what the scan saw — not a verdict on the site. The rescan after deployment is what shows whether tools are held back.
"
`;

